        "math_academy_id": "q-105033",
        "knowledge_point_id": "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d",
        "knowledge_point": "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets",
        "problem": "Which of the following are subsets of ${Q}_{0}^{-}$ (the set of non-positive rational numbers)?\n\nI. $Z$\n\nII. ${Z}^{-}$ (the set of negative integers)\n\nIII. $N$",
        "worked_solution": "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- $Z$ is *not* a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{-}.$ For example, $1\\in Z$ but $1\\notin {Q}_{0}^{-}.$\n\n- ${Z}^{-}$ is  a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{-}.$ Every negative integer is a non-positive rational number.\n\n- $N$ is *not* a subset of ${Q}_{0}^{-}.$ For example, $1\\in N$ but $1\\notin {Q}_{0}^{-}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
        "answer_fields": [
          {
