        "math_academy_id": "q-130031",
        "knowledge_point_id": "1d83ab99-0e27-5989-9a57-6b4a192fa4a4",
        "knowledge_point": "Computing a Probability at a Point",
        "problem": "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.4),$ compute $P(X=5).$  Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places.",
        "worked_solution": "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.4),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(0.6)}^{x-1}(0.4), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & ={(0.6)}^{4}(0.4) \\\\  & ≈0.0518.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
