      {
        "math_academy_id": "q-158447",
        "knowledge_point_id": "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa",
        "knowledge_point": "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF",
        "problem": "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k},\\, & 1<x<4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "worked_solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{k}{\\int }_{1}^{4}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}([\\frac{64}{3}+4]-[\\frac{1}{3}+1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}(21+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{k}=1\\,⟹\\,k=24.\n$$",
        "answer_fields": [
