      {
        "math_academy_id": "q-165040",
        "knowledge_point_id": "b4636870-89db-5bf9-8a04-777407957e33",
        "knowledge_point": "Finding Points Where a Tangent Vector is Parallel to Another Vector",
        "problem": "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-2t\\,,\\,{t}^{2}\\,,\\,8t-2{t}^{2}⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩.$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8t-2{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c\\,⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c\\,⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t⟩ & =c\\,⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t⟩ & =⟨c\\,,\\,2c\\,,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2t-2=c \\\\ 2t=2c \\\\ 8-4t=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we obtain that $t=2.$ Substituting to the second equation, we get $c=2.$\nThese values also satisfy the first equation. \n\nTherefore, $⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨{2}^{2}-2(2)\\,,\\,{2}^{2}\\,,\\,8(2)-2(2)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,4,\\,8⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(0,4,8).$",
        "answer_fields": [
          {
