      {
        "math_academy_id": "q-172690",
        "knowledge_point_id": "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b",
        "knowledge_point": "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets",
        "problem": "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [0,i].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$",
        "worked_solution": "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=\\{1\\}\\times [0,1]=\\{(1,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/71/71dd97607df48caf579c2e476520507ede035f65c336064f022cd900b6c120fc.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{i\\}\\times [0,i]=\\{(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]\\}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,i).$ \n\n![](images/4b/4b87c830620f54e5b3a3bb7923be971b4d9d4abb98cfb3dcdc8387f376a0b452.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set.",
        "answer_fields": [
          {
            "key": "selection",
            "type": "radio",
            "choices_complete": true,
            "choices": [
              {
                "type": "image",
                "value": "images/50/506d86848903de9f038d541152aabf67d99d0f4d0a7e5f5772b811ff1cf9dade.png",
                "option": "0",
