      {
        "math_academy_id": "q-26094",
        "knowledge_point_id": "90455606-1080-552e-a825-0cc7054b4be3",
        "knowledge_point": "Solving a System Containing Fractions in One Equation Using Substitution",
        "problem": "Find the solution to the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{6}+\\frac{5y}{6}-2=0 \\\\ 3x+y+6=0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "worked_solution": "To get rid of the fractions in the first equation, we must multiply the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $6.$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{6}+\\frac{5y}{6}-2=0 & |\\,\\times 6 \\\\ 3x+y+6=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x+5y-12=0 \\\\ 3x+y+6=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Notice that the second equation can easily be solved for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y+6 & =0 \\\\ y & =-3x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $y=-3x-6$ into the first equation and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5y-12 & =0 \\\\ x+5(-3x-6)-12 & =0 \\\\ x-15x-30-12 & =0 \\\\ -14x-42 & =0 \\\\ -14x & =42 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $y,$ we substitute $x=-3$ into the equation $y=-3x-6$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x-6 \\\\  & =-3(-3)-6 \\\\  & =9-6 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-3,\\,y=3.$",
        "answer_fields": [
          {
            "key": "selection",
            "type": "radio",
            "choices": [
