        "knowledge_point_id": "be42e4b3-4b15-589c-a7e1-23cc07083aff",
        "knowledge_point": "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Given the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors",
        "problem": "Consider the standard basis $\\{i,j\\}$ of $\\,{R}^{2}.$ If $T$ is a linear transformation such that $T(i)=i$ and $T(j)=3j,$ then what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $T?$",
        "worked_solution": "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $\\,i\\,$ and  $\\,j.$\n\n![](images/f2/f2438da7f6f0a2ab7e6653cad7776ef7d01faba55d22a480a98c152e81f0d78a.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that $T(i)=i$ and $T(j)=3j,$ we can draw the image of the square under the transformation $T:$\n\n![](images/86/8674e7345c903bf193e09cc9ae1702383cad10ba06c5dc3b6d2713ea30eb843e.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch with a scale factor $3$ in the $y$-direction.",
        "answer_fields": [
          {
            "key": "selection",
            "type": "radio",
            "choices_complete": true,
