      {
        "math_academy_id": "q-42886",
        "knowledge_point_id": "0e2a8cf2-64db-5b2f-b0f7-0c727cf492a9",
        "knowledge_point": "Describing Properties of the Secant Function",
        "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x?$\n\nI. The point $(0,2)$ is in the graph\n\nII. $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote\n\nIII. $y=-2$ belongs to the range of the function.",
        "worked_solution": "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec⁡x.$\n\n![](images/da/da729794a0331383debd75be0a4ce4a9f2d4da3286f50bd4f284868996077412.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The $y$-intercept of $sec⁡x$ is $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function $y=sec⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ thus $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote.\n\n- Statement III is true. The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ so $y=-2$ belongs to the range.",
        "answer_fields": [
