      {
        "math_academy_id": "q-66669",
        "knowledge_point_id": "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d",
        "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description",
        "problem": "Given that $f(x)=(x-2)^{2},$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nI. $x\\in (2,\\infty )$\n\nII. $x\\in (-1,5)$\n\nIII. $x\\in (-\\infty ,2]$",
        "worked_solution": "The graph of the function $f(x)=(x-2)^{2},$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n1\\. Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it by $2$ units  to  the right  $f(x)=(x-2)^{2}.$\n\n![](images/1e/1e3ac550e35a3dc93b3e26e5834f763847b04f5133de02e368124f54dd2b9dd6.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-1,5).$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/3e/3ebb316ca122b6786b8ef501fc7c56f8e0e68c8cfc32e5801c493d38382fc26c.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the intervals $x\\in (2,\\infty )$  and $x\\in (-\\infty ,2].$  On these intervals, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/86/86c7c9cacc7a3868565f33130563ffed0ca423aa087e76cc08d103ea171822ed.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\".",
        "answer_fields": [
          {
            "key": "selection",
            "type": "radio",
            "choices": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "value": "III only",
                "option": "0",
