      {
        "math_academy_id": "q-74421",
        "knowledge_point_id": "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03",
        "knowledge_point": "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point",
        "problem": "![](images/67/67d09cf1ebb51a37c122ea1e0bb53b0bcabedca0251ae4105ebb93dd55d111dd.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8$ that is closest to the point $(-4,-1).$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8$ and passes through the point $(-4,-1).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x+4) \\\\ y+1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8 \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x-8 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =8-\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{13}{3}\\cdot \\frac{6}{13} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-5.$ So, our solution is $x=2$ and $y=-5.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(2,-5).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8$ that is closest to the point $(-4,-1)$ is $(2,-5).$",
