      {
        "math_academy_id": "q-74626",
        "knowledge_point_id": "b010a518-7aa3-5b53-9a6d-ef0779ff0dfa",
        "knowledge_point": "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a Singular 3x3 System With Two Pivots",
        "problem": "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-2z=1 \\\\ -3x-6y+6z=-3 \\\\ 2x+5y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system.",
        "worked_solution": "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTransforming $M$ into reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -6 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -10 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-10z=-1 \\\\ y+4z=1 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation, we can remove this equation from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=10z-1 \\\\ y=1-4z \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10z-1 \\\\ 1-4z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "answer_fields": [
          {
            "key": "selection",
            "type": "radio",
            "choices_complete": true,
            "choices": [
              {
                "type": "math",
                "value": "[\\begin{aligned}5z-1 \\\\ 1-2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )",
                "option": "0",
                "source_option": "a"
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "value": "[\\begin{aligned}10z-1 \\\\ 1-4z \\\\ z+1\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )",
                "option": "1",
                "source_option": "b"
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "value": "[\\begin{aligned}10z+1 \\\\ 1+4z \\\\ z+1\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )",
                "option": "2",
                "source_option": "c"
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "value": "[\\begin{aligned}5z-1 \\\\ 1-4z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )",
                "option": "3",
                "source_option": "d"
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "value": "[\\begin{aligned}10z-1 \\\\ 1-4z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )",
                "option": "4",
                "source_option": "e"
              }
            ],
            "correct_value": "[\\begin{aligned}10z-1 \\\\ 1-4z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )",
            "value_type": "math",
