      {
        "math_academy_id": "q-75730",
        "knowledge_point_id": "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb",
        "knowledge_point": "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors",
        "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =4.$\n\nII. An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$\n\nIII. If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5,$ then $-v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =5.$",
        "worked_solution": "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=\\frac{1}{4}v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{4}{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $4$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=4v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $4.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(-v)=-Av=-(5v)=5(-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(-v)=5(-v),$ and $-v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\".",
        "answer_fields": [
          {
            "key": "selection",
            "type": "radio",
            "choices_complete": true,
