        "knowledge_point": "Identifying a Shear or Stretch Transformation Given the Images of the Standard Basis Vectors",
        "problem": "Consider the standard basis $\\{i,j\\}$ of ${R}^{2}.$ If $T$ is a linear transformation such that $T(i)=\\frac{1}{2}i$ and $T(j)=j,$ then what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $T?$",
        "worked_solution": "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $i$ and  $j.$\n\n![](images/34/34c82826da26290fc890671734c354fbfd9c253f1cfaca38de1ea615434e6766.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that $T(i)=\\frac{1}{2}i$ and $T(j)=j,$ we can draw the image of the square under the transformation $T:$\n\n![](images/02/02470083dea4808af1854e9ed88ab496828a429c593af780209d8a6d4b15a6b1.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch with a scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $x$-direction.",
        "answer_fields": [
