      {
        "math_academy_id": "q-81823",
        "knowledge_point_id": "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b",
        "knowledge_point": "Identifying Rotation Matrices",
        "problem": "![](images/0c/0c2f0240b962701c2eb2ee34a0eddc164afb8e1a677a5ed7f81fe65efb890358.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
        "worked_solution": "Consider the standard basis $\\{i,j\\}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of $\\{i,j\\}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/4e/4e0e5c9cee037bded1f5739af35c3a8acfaa4d4e6f469b3e50afe69b96990e39.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/57/578b3315028bf5a19a4b6e17d228db08b41f24dee63e5cb7f8d601ce667785f4.png)",
        "answer_fields": [
