      {
        "math_academy_id": "q-84108",
        "knowledge_point_id": "7511fb86-dea4-5206-9ccd-69628a59473e",
        "knowledge_point": "Parametrizing a Vertical Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin",
        "problem": "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-3,-3)?$",
        "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-3,-3),$ we can substitute $x=-3$ and $y=-3$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3=2pt \\\\ -3=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =p{(-\\frac{3}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ -3 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{9}{4p} \\\\ 4p & =-3 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{3}{4})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{3}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$",
        "answer_fields": [
