      {
        "math_academy_id": "q-90174",
        "knowledge_point_id": "0cd1bb61-2770-5096-83d6-d1e4a7189e9a",
        "knowledge_point": "Working With Systems Containing \"Or Equal To\" Inequalities",
        "problem": "Which of the diagrams shows the solution of the following system?\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y\\le 2x \\\\ y\\le -2x\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "worked_solution": "To graph the first inequality $y\\le 2x,$ we draw the corresponding straight line $y=2x.$\n\n- Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, the line itself is included in the solution, so the line is solid.\n\n- The inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* or equal to those on the line, so we shade *below* the line.\n\nTo graph the second inequality $y\\le -2x,$ we draw the corresponding straight line $y=-2x.$ \n\n- Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, the line itself is included in the solution, so the line is solid.\n\n- The inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* or equal to those on the line, so we shade *below* the line.\n\nThe intersection of the solutions is the following:\n\n![](images/43/438928da5e9de5e9ba3d2abbe06b6f1f8b6b0598a0b82bec1f51824b9ef72a71.png)",
        "answer_fields": [
